    {
     "author": {
      "accountId": "5fca8c79aca10c0069e36e33",
      "accountType": "atlassian",
      "active": true,
      "avatarUrls": {
       "16x16": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/64bf6f26854d620d318d4082f2e6a6a7?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FE-3.png",
       "24x24": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/64bf6f26854d620d318d4082f2e6a6a7?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FE-3.png",
       "32x32": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/64bf6f26854d620d318d4082f2e6a6a7?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FE-3.png",
       "48x48": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/64bf6f26854d620d318d4082f2e6a6a7?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FE-3.png"
      },
      "displayName": "eddie",
      "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=5fca8c79aca10c0069e36e33",
      "timeZone": "America/Halifax"
     },
     "body": "<p>Why is the UploadResource class located in org.fcrepo.server.upload rather than in org.fcrepo.server.rest (where the rest of the REST API impls are)?</p>\n\n<p>Also, the REST API documentation states that the service endpoint is /objects/upload but it's actually at /upload. Without consideration for implementation, is one preferable over the other? Or something else entirely?</p>\n\n<p>Finally, API-M-Lite's upload returned a 201, but this implementation returns a 202. What's the rationale for returning a 202? (Not necessarily objecting, but it would be good to document at least in this ticket the reasoning). I can see how a 201 seems a bit inappropriate since \"The newly created resource can be referenced by the URI(s) returned in the entity of the response\" and we're returning an \"uploaded://\" URI. However a 202 seems at least as inappropriate: \"The 202 response is intentionally non-committal.... The entity returned with this response SHOULD include an indication of the request's current status and either a pointer to a status monitor or some estimate of when the user can expect the request to be fulfilled.\"</p>\n\n<p>Perhaps a 200 is the more appropriate response code so long as the documentation explains we're returning an uploaded:// uri that can be passed to Fedora as a dsLocation?</p>",
     "created": "07/Jul/10 12:13 PM",
     "id": "11470",
     "jsdPublic": true,
     "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/issue/10611/comment/11470",
